Guided Exercise: Application High Availability with Kubernetes
Simulate different types of application failures and observe how Kubernetes handles them.
Outcomes
Explore how the restartPolicy attribute affects crashing pods.
Observe the behavior of a slow-starting application that has no configured probes.
Use a deployment to scale the application, and observe the behavior of a broken pod.
As the student user on the workstation machine, use the lab command to prepare your system for this exercise.

1. Lab Setup
•	You start the lab environment:
lab start reliability-ha
What it does:
•	Creates the reliability-ha project in OpenShift.
•	Provides the YAML resource files and the long-load container image, which has endpoints to simulate crashes and unhealthy states.
•	Log in as the developer user:
oc login -u developer -p developer https://api.ocp4.example.com:6443
oc project reliability-ha
You are now working in the reliability-ha project and ready to create pods.
________________________________________
2. Create a Pod with restartPolicy: Always
YAML manifest (long-load.yaml):
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: long-load
spec:
  containers:
  - image: registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/redhattraining/long-load:v1
    name: long-load
    securityContext:
      allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
  restartPolicy: Always
•	Create pod:
oc apply -f long-load.yaml
•	Check pod is running:
oc exec long-load -- curl -s localhost:3000/health
# Ok
What you’re learning:
•	restartPolicy: Always ensures Kubernetes restarts the pod if it crashes.
•	This simulates automatic recovery in HA.
________________________________________
3. Crash the Pod
•	Trigger the pod to crash using the app’s /destruct endpoint:
oc exec long-load -- curl -s localhost:3000/destruct
•	Observe pod restart count:
oc get pods
Observation:
•	The pod restarts once automatically due to restartPolicy: Always.
Theory: HA works because Kubernetes detects container failure and restarts it automatically.
________________________________________
4. Delete the Pod Manually
oc delete pod long-load
Observation:
•	Pod is not re-created because you manually created it outside of a Deployment.
•	Key idea: Only higher-level controllers (Deployment/StatefulSet) guarantee persistent HA.
________________________________________
5. restartPolicy: Never
•	Modify YAML:
restartPolicy: Never
•	Apply:
oc apply -f long-load.yaml
•	Crash pod with /destruct:
oc exec long-load -- curl -s localhost:3000/destruct
Observation:
•	Pod does NOT restart. Status shows Error.
Theory: Never disables automatic restart, showing why Always is preferred for HA workloads.
________________________________________
6. Simulate Slow-Starting Application
•	Add a startup delay using environment variable:
env:
  - name: START_DELAY
    value: "60000"  # 60 seconds
•	Keep restartPolicy: Always.
•	Apply pod and test:
oc apply -f long-load.yaml
oc exec long-load -- curl -s localhost:3000/health
# app is still starting
•	After 60 seconds:
oc exec long-load -- curl -s localhost:3000/health
# Ok
Theory:
•	Without probes, Kubernetes thinks the pod is ready immediately, even if the app is still starting.
•	Startup delays or startup probes fix this by telling the cluster when the app is actually ready.
________________________________________
7. Using a Deployment for Scaling
•	Deployment YAML (long-load-deploy.yaml) defines:
spec:
  replicas: 3
  containers:
  - image: registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/redhattraining/long-load:v1
    env:
    - name: START_DELAY
      value: "15000"  # 15 seconds
•	Apply deployment:
oc apply -f long-load-deploy.yaml
•	Load-test the application:
./load-test.sh
•	Put one pod into broken state:
curl long-load-reliability-ha.apps.ocp4.example.com/togglesick
Observation:
•	Some requests fail (app is unhealthy), but the Deployment ensures other pods continue serving traffic.
•	When a pod crashes, Deployment recreates it, showing HA in action at a multi-pod level.
________________________________________
8. Finish Lab
lab finish reliability-ha
•	Cleans up resources to avoid affecting future labs.
________________________________________
Key Takeaways (Theory)
1.	restartPolicy: Controls pod auto-restart (Always, OnFailure, Never).
2.	Pod vs Deployment: Pod alone = manual HA; Deployment = automated HA.
3.	Health & Startup delays: Probes or delays prevent routing traffic to unready pods.
4.	Scaling & Load Balancing: Multiple replicas allow service continuity even if one pod fails.
5.	Simulated failures: Using /destruct and /togglesick endpoints demonstrates Kubernetes HA in real scenarios.
